# Paths that ask for a review before they change.
#
# Not distrust -- each of these fails in a way a diff does not show. A merge
# rule that looks fine loses data on the second device; a workflow edit runs
# with signing keys in scope; a rules change is the only thing between a bug and
# every user's library.
#
# IMPORTANT: this file is advisory on its own. GitHub only *requires* the review
# it requests when branch protection on `main` has "Require review from Code
# Owners" turned on. Without that setting these lines add a reviewer and nothing
# more.

# The cross-device merge. A whole-document overwrite here cost seven games and
# 56 priority edits in September 2026; tests/two-sessions.test.mjs exists
# because of it.
/src/services/syncMerge.js      @NishilVani
/src/services/db.js             @NishilVani

# COMPAT_LEVEL gates cloud writes for every client. Raising it stops older
# builds syncing -- see CONTRIBUTING.md.
/src/services/compat.js         @NishilVani

# The only thing standing between a bug and every user's data.
/firestore.rules                @NishilVani

# These run with Android signing keys, a Windows certificate and deploy
# credentials in scope.
/.github/workflows/             @NishilVani
/.github/CODEOWNERS             @NishilVani

# Holds the IGDB credential. Its endpoint allowlist is what stops it being an
# open credential for the whole of IGDB.
/functions/                     @NishilVani

# Structurally prevent CI from arming the compatibility gate. The guarantee is
# in how these are written, so a rewrite can quietly remove it.
/scripts/app_config_payload.mjs @NishilVani
/scripts/publish_app_config.mjs @NishilVani
